# Coffee Marketplace Platform - Software Engineering Project

## Project Title
**CoffeeConnect: Multi-Vendor E-Commerce Platform for Coffee Products and Services**

## Context and Problem Statement

### Context
The coffee industry faces challenges in connecting specialty coffee vendors, roasters, and equipment suppliers directly with consumers and businesses. Traditional e-commerce platforms lack specialized features for the coffee trade, such as subscription management, quality ratings specific to coffee characteristics, and business-to-business procurement capabilities.

### Problem Statement
There is a need for a comprehensive digital platform that:
- Enables coffee vendors to manage and sell their products/services online
- Provides consumers with access to specialty coffee products from multiple vendors
- Facilitates enterprise procurement for businesses requiring bulk orders
- Supports subscription-based models for regular coffee deliveries
- Ensures quality control through specialized review and rating systems

## Objectives

### General Objective
Develop a secure, scalable, and user-friendly multi-vendor e-commerce platform specialized for the coffee industry, supporting B2C and B2B transactions.

### Specific Objectives
1. Design and implement a role-based access system (Admin, Vendor, Enterprise Client, Individual Client)
2. Create a vendor management system with product/service catalog and analytics
3. Implement a shopping cart and order management system
4. Develop a subscription management module for recurring orders
5. Build an administrative dashboard for platform management
6. Integrate a review and rating system specific to coffee products
7. Implement real-time analytics for vendors and administrators
8. Ensure responsive design for mobile and desktop access

## Functional Requirements

### FR1: User Authentication and Authorization
- **FR1.1**: Users must register with email, password, and role selection
- **FR1.2**: System must support four user roles: Admin, Vendor, Enterprise Client, Individual Client
- **FR1.3**: Users must be able to log in and log out securely
- **FR1.4**: Password recovery mechanism must be available

### FR2: Product Management (Vendor)
- **FR2.1**: Vendors must add products with name, description, price, category, and images
- **FR2.2**: Vendors must edit and delete their products
- **FR2.3**: Products must be categorized (Coffee Beans, Equipment, Accessories, etc.)
- **FR2.4**: Vendors must manage inventory levels and stock status
- **FR2.5**: Products can be marked as featured or on promotion

### FR3: Category Management (Admin)
- **FR3.1**: Admins must create, edit, and delete product categories
- **FR3.2**: Categories must support hierarchical organization (parent/subcategories)
- **FR3.3**: Each category must have a name, description, and icon/image

### FR4: Shopping and Cart Management
- **FR4.1**: Clients must browse products by category, vendor, or search
- **FR4.2**: Clients must add products to cart with quantity selection
- **FR4.3**: Clients must view and modify cart contents before checkout
- **FR4.4**: System must calculate total price including taxes

### FR5: Order Management
- **FR5.1**: Clients must complete orders with shipping information
- **FR5.2**: System must generate unique order numbers
- **FR5.3**: Vendors must view and manage orders for their products
- **FR5.4**: Order status must be tracked (Pending, Processing, Shipped, Delivered, Cancelled)
- **FR5.5**: Clients must view their order history

### FR6: Subscription Management
- **FR6.1**: Clients must subscribe to recurring coffee deliveries
- **FR6.2**: Subscription plans must support different frequencies (weekly, monthly)
- **FR6.3**: Clients must manage (pause, cancel, modify) their subscriptions
- **FR6.4**: System must automatically process subscription renewals

### FR7: Review and Rating System
- **FR7.1**: Clients must rate products (1-5 stars)
- **FR7.2**: Clients must write detailed reviews with text feedback
- **FR7.3**: Reviews must include criteria specific to coffee (aroma, taste, body, acidity)
- **FR7.4**: System must calculate average ratings for products

### FR8: Vendor Dashboard and Analytics
- **FR8.1**: Vendors must view sales analytics (revenue, orders, trends)
- **FR8.2**: Dashboard must display key metrics (monthly revenue, total orders)
- **FR8.3**: Vendors must access detailed sales reports with date filters
- **FR8.4**: System must provide product performance analytics

### FR9: Admin Dashboard
- **FR9.1**: Admins must view platform-wide statistics
- **FR9.2**: Admins must manage user accounts (approve vendors, manage clients)
- **FR9.3**: Admins must moderate product listings and reviews
- **FR9.4**: System must generate platform revenue and transaction reports

### FR10: Search and Filter
- **FR10.1**: Users must search products by name, description, or category
- **FR10.2**: Users must filter products by price range, rating, vendor, category
- **FR10.3**: Search results must be sorted by relevance, price, rating, or date

## Non-Functional Requirements

### NFR1: Performance
- **NFR1.1**: Page load time must not exceed 3 seconds
- **NFR1.2**: System must support at least 1000 concurrent users
- **NFR1.3**: Database queries must execute in less than 500ms
- **NFR1.4**: Product images must be optimized and lazy-loaded

### NFR2: Security
- **NFR2.1**: All passwords must be hashed using industry-standard algorithms
- **NFR2.2**: Authentication tokens must expire after 24 hours of inactivity
- **NFR2.3**: All API endpoints must validate user permissions
- **NFR2.4**: Sensitive data must be encrypted in transit (HTTPS) and at rest
- **NFR2.5**: SQL injection and XSS attacks must be prevented

### NFR3: Usability
- **NFR3.1**: Interface must be intuitive with minimal learning curve
- **NFR3.2**: System must provide clear error messages and validation feedback
- **NFR3.3**: Interface must be accessible (WCAG 2.1 Level AA compliance)
- **NFR3.4**: User actions must have visual feedback (loading states, confirmations)

### NFR4: Scalability
- **NFR4.1**: Architecture must support horizontal scaling
- **NFR4.2**: Database must handle growing product catalog (100,000+ products)
- **NFR4.3**: System must support adding new vendor accounts without performance degradation

### NFR5: Maintainability
- **NFR5.1**: Code must follow TypeScript and React best practices
- **NFR5.2**: Components must be modular and reusable
- **NFR5.3**: System must have comprehensive documentation
- **NFR5.4**: Code must have minimum 70% test coverage

### NFR6: Compatibility
- **NFR6.1**: System must work on modern browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- **NFR6.2**: Interface must be responsive (mobile, tablet, desktop)
- **NFR6.3**: System must support screen sizes from 320px to 4K displays

### NFR7: Availability
- **NFR7.1**: System uptime must be 99.5% or higher
- **NFR7.2**: Planned maintenance windows must not exceed 4 hours
- **NFR7.3**: System must have automated backup every 24 hours

## Actors and Use Cases

### Actors

#### 1. Individual Client (Consumer)
- **Description**: End user purchasing coffee products for personal consumption
- **Responsibilities**: Browse products, make purchases, manage orders, leave reviews, manage subscriptions

#### 2. Enterprise Client (Business)
- **Description**: Business entity making bulk purchases for organizational needs
- **Responsibilities**: Browse products, place bulk orders, manage enterprise account, view invoices, manage contracts

#### 3. Vendor
- **Description**: Coffee producer, roaster, or equipment supplier selling on the platform
- **Responsibilities**: Manage product catalog, process orders, view analytics, manage inventory, respond to reviews

#### 4. Administrator
- **Description**: Platform manager with full system access
- **Responsibilities**: Manage users, categories, moderate content, view platform analytics, configure system settings

### Use Cases

#### UC1: User Registration
- **Actor**: Individual Client, Enterprise Client, Vendor
- **Description**: New user creates an account on the platform
- **Preconditions**: User has valid email address
- **Main Flow**:
  1. User navigates to registration page
  2. User provides email, password, and selects role
  3. User provides additional information based on role
  4. System validates input
  5. System creates account and sends confirmation email
  6. User confirms email and account is activated
- **Postconditions**: User account is created and active

#### UC2: Add Product (Vendor)
- **Actor**: Vendor
- **Description**: Vendor adds a new product to their catalog
- **Preconditions**: Vendor is authenticated and has an active account
- **Main Flow**:
  1. Vendor navigates to product management
  2. Vendor clicks "Add Product"
  3. Vendor enters product details (name, description, price, category)
  4. Vendor uploads product images
  5. Vendor sets inventory level
  6. System validates input
  7. System saves product to database
  8. System displays confirmation message
- **Postconditions**: New product is added to vendor's catalog

#### UC3: Search and Browse Products
- **Actor**: Individual Client, Enterprise Client
- **Description**: User searches for and browses available products
- **Preconditions**: None (public access)
- **Main Flow**:
  1. User enters search query or selects category
  2. System retrieves matching products
  3. User applies filters (price, rating, vendor)
  4. System updates results based on filters
  5. User views product details
- **Postconditions**: User has viewed relevant products

#### UC4: Place Order
- **Actor**: Individual Client, Enterprise Client
- **Description**: User completes a purchase
- **Preconditions**: User is authenticated, cart has items
- **Main Flow**:
  1. User reviews cart contents
  2. User proceeds to checkout
  3. User provides shipping information
  4. User selects payment method
  5. User confirms order
  6. System processes payment
  7. System creates order record
  8. System sends confirmation to user and vendor
  9. System decrements inventory
- **Postconditions**: Order is created and vendors are notified

#### UC5: Manage Subscription
- **Actor**: Individual Client
- **Description**: User creates or modifies a coffee subscription
- **Preconditions**: User is authenticated
- **Main Flow**:
  1. User navigates to subscription management
  2. User selects subscription plan
  3. User chooses frequency and products
  4. User provides payment and shipping information
  5. System creates subscription
  6. System schedules recurring orders
- **Alternate Flow**: User pauses or cancels existing subscription
- **Postconditions**: Subscription is active or modified

#### UC6: Manage Categories (Admin)
- **Actor**: Administrator
- **Description**: Admin creates, edits, or deletes product categories
- **Preconditions**: Admin is authenticated
- **Main Flow**:
  1. Admin navigates to category management
  2. Admin clicks "Add Category"
  3. Admin enters category name, description, and parent category
  4. Admin uploads category icon
  5. System validates input
  6. System saves category
  7. System updates category hierarchy
- **Postconditions**: Category structure is updated

#### UC7: View Analytics (Vendor)
- **Actor**: Vendor
- **Description**: Vendor views sales and performance analytics
- **Preconditions**: Vendor is authenticated
- **Main Flow**:
  1. Vendor navigates to analytics dashboard
  2. Vendor selects date range
  3. System retrieves sales data
  4. System displays charts and metrics
  5. Vendor can export reports
- **Postconditions**: Vendor has reviewed performance data

#### UC8: Write Product Review
- **Actor**: Individual Client, Enterprise Client
- **Description**: Customer leaves a review for purchased product
- **Preconditions**: User has purchased and received the product
- **Main Flow**:
  1. User navigates to order history
  2. User selects product to review
  3. User provides star rating (1-5)
  4. User writes review text
  5. User rates coffee characteristics (aroma, taste, body, acidity)
  6. System validates input
  7. System saves review
  8. System updates product average rating
- **Postconditions**: Review is published and visible to other users

## Proposed Architecture

### Architecture Pattern
**Three-Tier Architecture with Component-Based Frontend**

### Tier 1: Presentation Layer (Frontend)
- **Technology**: React 18 with TypeScript
- **State Management**: React Query for server state, React hooks for local state
- **Routing**: React Router v6
- **UI Framework**: Tailwind CSS with shadcn/ui components
- **Build Tool**: Vite

**Components Structure**:
```
src/
├── components/
│   ├── ui/           # Reusable UI components (Button, Card, Dialog, etc.)
│   ├── layout/       # Layout components (Header, Footer, Sidebar)
│   ├── profile/      # Profile-related components
│   ├── marketplace/  # Marketplace components (ProductGrid, Filters)
│   ├── subscription/ # Subscription management components
│   ├── analytics/    # Analytics and dashboard components
│   └── reviews/      # Review and rating components
├── pages/            # Page components (mapped to routes)
├── hooks/            # Custom React hooks
└── lib/              # Utility functions and helpers
```

### Tier 2: Application Layer (Backend)
- **Technology**: Supabase (PostgreSQL + Edge Functions)
- **Authentication**: Supabase Auth with JWT
- **Storage**: Supabase Storage for product images
- **API**: RESTful API via Supabase

**Edge Functions** (Serverless):
- Payment processing
- Order creation and validation
- Email notifications
- Subscription management
- Analytics aggregation

### Tier 3: Data Layer
- **Database**: PostgreSQL (via Supabase)
- **ORM**: Supabase Client Library
- **Caching**: Browser cache + Service Workers

**Database Schema** (Main Tables):
- `users` (id, email, role, profile_data)
- `products` (id, vendor_id, name, description, price, category_id, inventory, images)
- `categories` (id, name, description, parent_id, icon)
- `orders` (id, client_id, total, status, shipping_info, created_at)
- `order_items` (id, order_id, product_id, quantity, price)
- `subscriptions` (id, client_id, plan_id, status, frequency, next_billing)
- `reviews` (id, product_id, user_id, rating, comment, coffee_ratings)
- `vendors` (id, user_id, business_name, description, rating, total_sales)

### Security Architecture
- **Authentication**: JWT tokens with refresh mechanism
- **Authorization**: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
- **Data Protection**: Row Level Security (RLS) policies in PostgreSQL
- **API Security**: Rate limiting, input validation, CORS configuration

### Deployment Architecture
- **Frontend Hosting**: Lovable/Vercel/Netlify (CDN)
- **Backend**: Supabase Cloud
- **CI/CD**: Git-based deployment pipeline
- **Monitoring**: Application performance monitoring, error tracking

## UML Diagrams (Expected Deliverables)

### 1. Use Case Diagram
**Purpose**: Illustrate system functionality from user perspective
- All actors (Admin, Vendor, Enterprise Client, Individual Client)
- Main use cases for each actor
- Relationships between use cases (include, extend, generalization)

### 2. Class Diagram
**Purpose**: Show static structure of the system
- Main classes: User, Product, Order, Subscription, Review, Category, Vendor
- Attributes and methods for each class
- Relationships (association, aggregation, composition, inheritance)
- Multiplicities

### 3. Sequence Diagrams (Minimum 3)
**Purpose**: Show interaction between objects over time

**Required Diagrams**:
- **3.1**: User authentication flow
- **3.2**: Product purchase process (from cart to order confirmation)
- **3.3**: Subscription creation and renewal
- **3.4**: Product review submission

### 4. Activity Diagrams (Minimum 2)
**Purpose**: Show workflow of business processes

**Required Diagrams**:
- **4.1**: Order processing workflow (vendor perspective)
- **4.2**: Product search and filter process

### 5. State Diagram (Minimum 2)
**Purpose**: Show states and transitions of key objects

**Required Diagrams**:
- **5.1**: Order lifecycle states
- **5.2**: Subscription states

### 6. Component Diagram
**Purpose**: Show system components and dependencies
- Frontend components (React modules)
- Backend services (Edge Functions)
- External systems (Supabase, Payment Gateway)
- Component interfaces and dependencies

### 7. Deployment Diagram
**Purpose**: Show physical deployment of system
- Client devices (Browser)
- Web server / CDN
- Application server (Edge Functions)
- Database server
- File storage
- Network connections

### 8. Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD)
**Purpose**: Show database structure
- All entities (tables)
- Attributes and primary keys
- Relationships and foreign keys
- Cardinalities

### 9. Package Diagram
**Purpose**: Show organization of code into packages
- Frontend packages (components, pages, hooks, utilities)
- Backend packages (functions, models, services)
- Dependencies between packages

